ly the canonical specification sections\nneeded for the one selected chapter. Treat SHARD-05 as held unless explicit\nauthorisation is recorded; do not turn a gate into approval.\n\n## Required behaviour\n\nSelect no more than one queued chapter whose dependencies and gates permit\nplanning. Add at most 250 stable `IMP-*` contracts with requirement links,\nowned files/data/interfaces, dependencies, non-goals, platform distinctions,\nverification, asset assessment, and canonical source links. Validate coverage,\nunique IDs, ownership, and acyclic dependencies. Allocate only ready\nunassigned contracts, using monotonic B/R counters and batches of no more than\nseven R IDs; materialise at most ten corresponding B runbooks. Each B writer\nmust contain a context packet, output list, validation procedure,\ncheckpoint/update procedure, collision stop rule, and stop condition. Do not\ncreate R-series files yourself.\n\n## Checkpoint and update procedure\n\nAtomically update the JSON/Markdown ledger counters, chapter state, contract\nallocations, and checkpoint after validation. Never renumber, replace, or\nrewrite an accepted B/R contract. If work remains, create exactly one new\ndispatcher with the next monotonic dispatcher ID and a later sequence. If no\nqueued chapter or ready contract remains, create exactly one final audit\nB-series runbook that validates all completed catalogue expansions and batches;\nit must not pretend to generate or review all work in this dispatch.\n\n## Validation procedure\n\nValidate JSON, the 250-contract chapter cap, no more than ten newly-created B\nfiles, no more than seven assigned R IDs per B file, unique B/R/IMP IDs,\ndependency closure, provenance values, asset metadata, output ownership, and\nthe successor/final-audit choice. Stop and report a source conflict, missing\ncoverage, cycle, ID/path collision, unresolved authority, or any need to\noverwrite accepted work.\n\n## Stop condition\n\nStop immediately after one chapter expansion (or none), one allocation wave,\nand exactly one successor dispatcher or final audit task. This is not a\nrecursive prompt and must not materialise a whole-game plan.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- This iteration expands at most one eligible chapter and adds no more than 250 IMP contracts.\n- It creates at most ten bounded B-series authoring tasks, each with at most seven assigned R-series outputs.\n- Every created B task has its context packet, output list, validation, checkpoint/update, collision protection, and stop condition.\n- If queued or ready work remains, exactly one successor dispatcher is created; otherwise exactly one final audit B-series task is created.\n- IDs, manifest ownership, provenance, asset assessments, and dependencies are validated without overwriting accepted work.\n---\ntask_id: D0002\nsequence: 9\ntitle: Resume bounded implementation-runbook authoring dispatch after gate review\nbrowser_impact: not_applicable\nplaywright_spec:\n---\n\n## Objective\n\nRun one durable authoring-dispatch iteration after D0001. Expand at most one\neligible queued specification/catalogue chapter into no more than 250 `IMP-*`\nrecords, validate it, then allocate ready unassigned contracts into at most ten\nnew B-series authoring tasks with no more than seven R-series outputs each.\nCreate exactly one successor dispatcher when queued work or ready contracts\nremain; otherwise create exactly one final audit B-series task. Exit after that\nbounded set.\n\n## Context packet\n\nRead `INITIAL.md`, `../PROJECT_BRIEF.md`, `../specification/README.md`,\n`../planning/implementation-catalogue-index.json`,\n`../planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.json`, its Markdown ledger, and\n`../../../runbooks/F014.md`. Review D0001's `dispatch_history` checkpoint and\nonly the canonical specification sections needed for a selected chapter.\n`SHARD-02` is not eligible until `DEC-0004`/`GATE-0004` is explicitly resolved;\n`SHARD-03` and `SHARD-04` retain their listed predecessor/gate blocks; and\n`SHARD-05` remains held unless explicit authorisation is recorded.\n\n## Output list\n\n- No R-series output is preassigned. Allocate R IDs only for ready, unassigned\n  catalogue contracts discovered after the bounded chapter check.\n- At most ten new B-series authoring runbooks, each owning no more than seven\n  newly assigned R-series outputs.\n- Exactly one successor dispatcher or one final audit B-series task, selected\n  only after the queue and ready-contract check.\n\n## Required behaviour\n\nPreserve all accepted work and use monotonic B/R/IMP IDs. Do not infer approval\nfrom a gate or from a queued state. A selected expansion must include stable\nrequirement links, ownership, dependencies, non-goals, platform distinctions,\nverification, asset assessment, and canonical sources; validate coverage,\nunique IDs, ownership, provenance, and acyclic dependencies before allocation.\nEvery new B task must provide its context packet, output list, validation,\ncheckpoint/update procedure, collision protection, and stop condition. Do not\ncreate R-series files in this dispatcher.\n\n## Validation procedure\n\nValidate JSON, the selected chapter's 250-contract limit, unique IMP/B/R IDs,\ndependency closure and acyclicity, ownership/path collisions, allocation\nuniqueness, provenance, and asset metadata. Confirm each B owns at most seven R\nIDs and that no more than ten B tasks were created. Confirm the successor/final\naudit choice against the remaining queued chapters and ready unassigned\ncontracts. Stop and report any source conflict, missing coverage, cycle,\ncollision, unresolved authority, or need to overwrite accepted work.\n\n## Checkpoint and update procedure\n\nAtomically update the JSON and Markdown ledgers with chapter state, allocations,\ncounters, and the D0002 checkpoint only after validation. Retain reserved IDs\non repair; never renumber, replace, or rewrite an accepted B/R contract.\n\n## Collision protection\n\nBefore creating any B, R, IMP, successor, or audit path, verify that its ID and\npath are absent from the authoritative manifest and filesystem. On collision,\nstop without overwriting or reusing the ID.\n\n## Stop condition\n\nStop immediately after at most one chapter expansion, one allocation wave, and\nexactly one successor dispatcher or final audit task. This is not a recursive\nprompt and must not materialise a whole-game plan.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- The iteration expands at most one eligible chapter and adds no more than 250 IMP contracts.\n- It creates at most ten bounded B-series authoring tasks, each with at most seven assigned R-series outputs.\n- Every created B task has its context packet, output list, validation, checkpoint/update, collision protection, and stop condition.\n- If queued or ready work remains, exactly one successor dispatcher is created; otherwise exactly one final audit B-series task is created.\n- IDs, manifest ownership, provenance, asset assessments, and dependencies are validated without overwriting accepted work.\n# Runbook-authoring allocation ledger\n\nThis readable companion to [`runbook-authoring-manifest.json`](runbook-authoring-manifest.json) is the persistent allocation ledger for the mock-fantasy-quest-4 authoring system. The JSON is authoritative for IDs, provenance, dependencies, output paths, counters, and recovery state. Accepted IDs are immutable: resume from the first non-accepted batch and append only at `B0007` and `R0013`.\n\n## Dispatcher checkpoint \u2014 D0001\n\nD0001 expanded no chapter and allocated no B/R contracts. `SHARD-02` is the\nfirst queued chapter, but `DEC-0004`/`GATE-0004` remains unresolved; `SHARD-03`\nand `SHARD-04` retain their predecessor and gate blocks, while `SHARD-05` stays\nheld pending explicit authorisation. Every current catalogue contract\n(`IMP-0001` through `IMP-0012`) is allocated exactly once. The dispatcher\ntherefore created [`D0002`](../authoring-runbooks/D0002.md) as the sole\nsuccessor, without advancing B/R counters or modifying accepted work.\n\n## Wave 1 \u2014 foundation contracts\n\n| Order | Batch | R contracts (maximum 7) | Dependency / parallel disposition | Checkpoint |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | B0001 | R0001 | GATE-0001; no parallel work | Record gate and R0001 ownership |\n| 2 | B0002 | R0002, R0003, R0010 | After B0001 | Validate seams, fixtures, tokens and retained gates |\n| 3 | B0003 | R0004, R0008 | After B0002 | Validate state slice and P0 service exclusion |\n| 4 | B0004 | R0005, R0006 | After B0003; R0006 waits for GATE-0003 | Validate content/save separation |\n| 5 | B0005 | R0007, R0009, R0011 | After B0004; R0007/GATE-0003 and R0009/GATE-0005 remain blocked | Validate migration, diagnostics, provenance |\n| 6 | B0006 | R0012 | After B0003 and B0004; safe in parallel with B0005 after B0004 | Cross-check foundation documentation and gates |\n\nThe batches are deliberately smaller than seven where a contract is context-heavy (state, persistence, content) or tightly coupled to its predecessor. Together they form an incremental foundation vertical slice: reproducible baseline, shared seams/test harness, deterministic game state and P0 exclusion, content and recovery boundaries, then provenance/diagnostic controls and documented closure. They do not imply that the game is implemented.\n\n## Contract allocation and asset assessment\n\n| R ID | IMP source | Writer | Output | Asset assessment |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| R0001 | IMP-0001 | B0001 | `runbooks/R0001.md` | not applicable \u2014 tooling boundary only |\n| R0002 | IMP-0002 | B0002 | `runbooks/R0002.md` | not applicable \u2014 interfaces only |\n| R0003 | IMP-0003 | B0002 | `runbooks/R0003.md` | not applicable \u2014 deterministic fixtures only |\n| R0004 | IMP-0004 | B0003 | `runbooks/R0004.md` | not applicable \u2014 state envelopes only |\n| R0005 | IMP-0005 | B0004 | `runbooks/R0005.md` | not applicable \u2014 schema reserves later asset fields |\n| R0006 | IMP-0006 | B0004 | `runbooks/R0006.md` | not applicable \u2014 save/recovery protocol |\n| R0007 | IMP-0007 | B0005 | `runbooks/R0007.md` | not applicable \u2014 migrations only |\n| R0008 | IMP-0008 | B0003 | `runbooks/R0008.md` | not applicable \u2014 negative service scope |\n| R0009 | IMP-0009 | B0005 | `runbooks/R0009.md` | not applicable \u2014 local diagnostics only |\n| R0010 | IMP-0010 | B0002 | `runbooks/R0010.md` | not applicable \u2014 no final art/fonts/icons |\n| R0011 | IMP-0011 | B0005 | `runbooks/R0011.md` | not applicable \u2014 provenance control only |\n| R0012 | IMP-0012 | B0006 | `runbooks/R0012.md` | not applicable \u2014 documentation only |\n\nNo Wave 1 contract creates a product asset. Each contract still carries an explicit `asset_impact`, empty `asset_ids`, and an asset brief in the JSON; its author must prohibit incidental asset creation. A later asset-required contract must reserve stable product-specific IDs and a brief before assignment.\n\n## Author packet and resume rules\n\nEach B writer receives only the baseline terminology, target constraints, templates, authoring rules, and the named specification/catalogue records in its JSON `context_packet`. Every R record has canonical `source_specifications`, `source_catalogue_ids`, `authoring_batch`, and `factory_stages` provenance. Use [`runbooks/TEMPLATE.md`](../../../runbooks/TEMPLATE.md) plus the F014 metadata and asset requirements.\n\nAt every checkpoint, validate unique B/R IDs, output-path ownership, dependency closure, provenance, the batch maximum, asset assessment, and retained human gates. On failure, retain all assigned IDs, repair the bounded failed output, and resume that same batch. Never regenerate accepted contracts or renumber the ledger.\n\n## Queued expansion chapters\n\n| Chapter | Future allocation point | State |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| SHARD-02 \u2014 Campaign content and domain logic | Wave 2 after GATE-0004 | queued |\n| SHARD-03 \u2014 Session, UI, input, and accessibility | Wave 3 after SHARD-02 and GATE-0002/0003/0004 | queued |\n| SHARD-04 \u2014 PWA delivery, performance, operations, release evidence | Wave 4 after SHARD-02/03 and GATE-0002/0005/0007 | queued |\n| SHARD-05 \u2014 Connected-scope decision package | Only explicit authorisation after GATE-0006; no P0 R allocation | hold |\n\nExpand one queued chapter into catalogue records before allocating its R IDs. This preserves the catalogue\u2019s dependency graph and makes a future thousand-runbook programme resumable without changing accepted work.\n\n## Structural check\n\n```sh\nnode -e 'const m=require(\"./projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.json\"); const r=m.r_contracts, b=m.batches, ids=a=>new Set(a).size===a.length; if (!ids(r.map(x=>x.id)) || !ids(b.map(x=>x.id)) || r.length!==m.expected_runbook_count || b.some(x=>x.r_contract_ids.length>7) || r.some(x=>!x.source_specifications.length || !x.source_catalogue_ids.length || !x.authoring_batch || !x.factory_stages.length || ![\"required\",\"not_applicable\"].includes(x.asset_impact) || !x.output_path || (x.asset_impact===\"required\" && (!x.asset_ids.length || !x.asset_brief))) ) process.exit(1); console.log(\"runbook authoring ledger structural check passed\")'\n```\n\nexec\n/bin/zsh -lc \"tail -n 180 projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.json && sed -n '1,120p' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/README.md && find projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/authoring-runbooks -maxdepth 1 -type f -name '[A-Z]*.md' -printf '%f\\\\n' | sort | tail -n 20\" in /Users/simon/Dev/github/jakowicz/runbook-generator\n succeeded in 0ms:\n{\n  \"manifest_id\": \"mock-fantasy-quest-4-runbook-authoring-ledger\",\n  \"version\": 1,\n  \"purpose\": \"Persistent, append-only allocation ledger for bounded B-series authoring batches and immutable R-series implementation contracts. It allocates the current catalogue only; queued shards remain expansion work.\",\n  \"project_slug\": \"mock